<section id="federation-limits" class="paper-section">
  <div class="container paper-shell">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="section-kicker">Problem framing</span>
      <h2>Why current PKI federation falls short.</h2>
      <p>
        Existing federation mechanisms can establish cross-domain trust, but they do
        not provide a formal, governed way to reason about path quality as PKI
        topologies grow denser and cryptographic transitions become more demanding.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="limits-grid">
      <article class="limit-card">
        <h3>Complexity explosion</h3>
        <p>
          Mesh PKI and dense cross-certification cause the number of admissible
          certification paths to grow rapidly, making validation and governance
          increasingly difficult across many domains.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="limit-card">
        <h3>Heuristic validation</h3>
        <p>
          Many path-building approaches rely on tree traversal, local preferences, and
          heuristics that stop at a valid route instead of proving that the selected
          route is globally optimal.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="limit-card">
        <h3>Lack of trust governance</h3>
        <p>
          Trust is commonly inherited from hierarchies or broad anchors, rather than
          being selectively defined between issuing CAs and governed federation points.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="limit-card">
        <h3>No crypto agility awareness</h3>
        <p>
          Traditional federation models rarely score or prioritize paths according to
          classical, hybrid, and post-quantum cryptographic transitions, leaving
          crypto-agile interoperability under-specified.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

